{"name":"Fluorine","hex":"#8faa8c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fluorine","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol F) with an atomic number of 9. It is the lightest of the halogens, a pale yellow-green, highly reactive gas that attacks all metals.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fluorine"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fluorine","blocks":[{"text":"Fluorine is a chemical element; it has the symbol F and atomic number 9. It is the lightest halogen and exists at standard conditions as pale yellow diatomic gas. Fluorine is extremely reactive as it reacts with all other elements except for the light noble gases. Fluorine in its elemental form is highly toxic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Fluoro_liquido_a_-196%C2%B0C_1.jpg/330px-Fluoro_liquido_a_-196%C2%B0C_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fluorine"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Beta-fluorine_crystal_structure.gif/500px-Beta-fluorine_crystal_structure.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Beta-fluorine_crystal_structure.gif/500px-Beta-fluorine_crystal_structure.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animation showing the crystal structure of β-fluorine. F2 molecules on the faces of the unit cell have rotations constrained to a plane, while those in the corners can rotate freely.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeta-fluorine_crystal_structure.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Fluorite-270246.jpg/250px-Fluorite-270246.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Fluorite-270246.jp